  [#strong[Survey items and mean scores];], [#strong[Qualitative theme and integrated interpretation];], [#strong[Student reflection];], [#strong[Guest lecturer reflection];],
  [Q1 Material relevance \= 3.49 \
  Q2 International perspectives \= 3.23 \
  Q3 “GL is useless” \= 1.54],
  [Cultural exchange; professional development. \
  \
  Convergence: High Q1 and Q2 means align with students’ accounts of cultural exchange and the lecturer’s emphasis on diverse professional viewpoints. The low Q3 mean indicates disagreement with the claim that GL was useless.],
  [“#emph[We can engage in a cultural exchange];,” \
  \
  “#emph[To know our culture and broaden our international perspectives through knowing cultures from abroad];”],
  [MCL: “#emph[Different points of view and insights from different professionals];.”],
  [Q6 Motivation to learn \= 3.20 \
  Q12 Motivation in this course \= 2.25 \
  Q13 Speaking/vocabulary \= 3.20],
  [Improved language skills; professional development. \
  \
  Partial convergence/divergence: Q13 supports perceived language development, consistent with the student’s confidence account. However, the lower Q12 mean indicates that perceived language gains did not necessarily translate into stronger motivation for the course. The lecturer frames the program as a bridge to professional practice.],
  [“#emph[helped me be more confident in speaking English];.”],
  [MC: “#emph[Bridges academic knowledge with real-world practice, motivating students, and preparing them for future professional environments];.”],
  [Q7 Clear objectives/sequence \= 2.00 \
  Q9 Inconsistent lecturer coordination \= 3.00 \
  Q11 Smooth platform integration \= 2.00],
  [Instructional coordination; direct engagement. \
  \
  Complementarity: Q7 and Q11 indicate limited endorsement of clear sequencing and smooth platform integration, while Q9 \(negatively worded) indicates perceived inconsistency in coordination. The reflections favor direct contact; they contextualize the need for stronger engagement but do not directly explain the low Q7 score.],
  [“#emph[I hope the next class is in person];.”],
  [MLC: “#emph[Even though the technology is there, I like to deal with the students directly];.”],
  [Q14 Internet/device issues \= 3.20 \
  Q15 Platform switching \= 3.40 \
  Q18 Difficulty obtaining technical help \= 2.20],
  [Technical issues; platform disruption. \
  \
  Convergence: The high Q14 and Q15 means are corroborated by reports of frozen video and unstable connections. Q15 is the highest mean among these barriers, highlighting platform switching as a particularly salient disruption.],
  [“#emph[Sometimes the video froze, and we missed parts of the lecture];.”],
  [MLC: “#emph[The students’ internet connection was spotty, which made some of them have to leave the GMeet calls];.”],
  [Q4 Presentation style easy to understand \= 3.20 \
  Q16 Accent/speech speed difficulty \= 3.20],
  [Speech delivery. \
  \
  Complementarity: The student’s quote directly explains the Q16 difficulty with speech rate. The lecturer’s reflection adds online engagement and connectivity constraints; it does not directly refer to speech speed.],
  [“#emph[The lecturer spoke too quickly, and I could not keep up];.”],
  [MCL: “#emph[Because of the online format, some students are not fully engaged, probably because of slow internet connections and other related issues];.”],
  [Q5 Confidence interacting online \= 3.00 \
  Q19 Cultural differences made participation hesitant \= 2.20],
  [Lack of interaction; participation. \
  \
  Convergence with nuance: Both reflections emphasize interaction. Q5 indicates moderate confidence in online interaction, while the lower Q19 mean suggests cultural hesitation was not strongly endorsed overall, even though limited interaction remained a qualitative concern.],
  [“#emph[Some sessions may be too short or lack interaction, making it difficult for us students to fully engage or ask questions];.”],
  [MC: “#emph[Students need to interact with each other to learn more deeply and also to keep their interest];.”],
